    <p>The clearest sign that the market wants this strength is that it keeps inventing job titles for it. McKinsey argued years ago that the analytics translator, the person who bridges data teams and frontline managers, was a new must-have role, projecting demand of two to four million in the United States (<a href="https://www.mckinsey.com/capabilities/quantumblack/our-insights/analytics-translator" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">McKinsey, 2018</a>). Best Answer Hub tags each match with an AI outlook, and the translator roles skew human-led, because reading what a customer actually meant, or what two teams are really arguing about, is not in the data.</p>

    <div class="cav">The evidence</div>
    <h2 id="value">Why does connecting ideas across fields pay off?</h2>
    <p>Because the biggest ideas are usually recombinations, not inventions from nothing, and the Synthesizer is built for recombination. A landmark analysis of nearly eighteen million scientific papers found that the highest-impact work pairs a foundation of conventional, well-accepted ideas with one unusual combination reaching outside the field. Papers that blended the two hit a high-impact rate of 9.1%, against roughly 5% for the field as a whole, nearly double (<a href="https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.1240474" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Uzzi et al., Science, 2013</a>).</p>
